if you're looking to buy a block of land  regardless if it's for your next home  your dream home or even just the  development today's video will focus on  the key criteria you need to understand  to basically be able to purchase the  best block of land possible for you  what's going on guys welcome back to the  channel for those of you who don't know  me my name is david thomich i'm a  registered architect here in western  australia and today is a very very  special video not only are we filming in  a brand new set with two brand new  computers behind me we'll get to that  later we're also filming on a wednesday  so moving forward we're going to be  producing two videos a week one on  monday one on wednesday and these  wednesday videos are going to be a  little bit more of a general knowledge  information from my background as an  architect and how i can help you to be  able to better yourselves in that sector  so if you're looking for anything  architecture related this video every  single wednesday is exactly where you're  going to find it so in today's video  we're talking about buying lands and  we're talking about buying land  specifically in australia but it is  transferable across the world because  the principles are very much the same so  when you're looking to buy your block of  land obviously the first thing you want  to be looking at is your actual budget  your budget is going to define which  piece of land you can buy and which  piece of land you can't buy it's very  very simple and it doesn't really matter  what block of land you're looking at  that is going to be the number one  critical factor that changes everything  and will sway you to one block  potentially over another however when we  take out the factor of any sort of  budget constraints we can actually talk  about the fundamental architectural  principles that you really should be  looking out for and looking into to be  able to purchase the best block of land  to be able to build the best possible  thing on that bit of land so let's start  with the solar orientation solar  orientation basically in australia you  want to be looking for a block of land  that is elongated in such a way that the  north side is on the longest side  now because a lot of properties in  australia are very long and skinny that  means you want to be basically looking  at a block of land that faces the  longest length to north so that your  living areas can be proportioned on the  north side this basically allows you to  heat up your whole living spaces  throughout the day  naturally using the sun and keep your  southern side for your private sleeping  quarters which are generally designed to  be in a cooler climate and a cooler  condition compared to the living space  so you can basically sleep a lot more  comfortably at night time if you can  find a block of land that is perfectly  positioned with the long side facing  north this is the first thing that you  can tick off the list now when you've  found the block of land you want to also  make sure there's no restrictions on  that black land so as an example on this  block of land that i'm sitting on right  now there's a four and a half meter  easement to the back of the property for  all sewer services which basically means  i can't build anything over those last  four and a half meters except grass  bricks and maybe some sort of paving  anything that isn't permanent and can be  simply removed upon request chances are  if you're watching this video you're  probably either looking at building your  first residential home or your next  residential home most people looking to  build their dream residential home have  already built a feud so they know what's  going on in australia if you're looking  to purchase your first block of land  there's a lot of things that are  continuous across the board and you  really really want to make sure you're  aware of before you commit to any  blocker land so the prime example blocks  the land go up for sale in australia all  the time however what they don't tell  you is that every block of land is  subject to the r codes the residential  codes of either western australia  victoria new south wales or whatever  state you fall under most likely these  residential codes are applicable all  over the world so it is still applicable  to anybody around the world watching  this video one thing to keep in mind is  the fact that a block of land zoned r40  as an example has certain restrictions  of how close you can build to the  boundary you can't go ahead and build  boundary to boundary on all four corners  and just take up a hundred percent of  that land most of the time you either  have to have a certain amount of outdoor  living space which cuts it down to 40 of  usable or 60 unusable land depending  which way you want to look at it it also  cuts down how close to the boundary can  be at any given time and how far back  from the street you have to be  so imagine you bought a block of land 10  meters wide 20 meters long and all of a  sudden you have to come in two meters  from both sides and get pushed back six  meters from the street  you now have a 14 metre wide block by a  six meter wide block that you can  effectively build on this is something  you really really want to take into  consideration before you make any  commitments because if you can't fit  your dream home or your next home in  those restrictions that are already  presented to you you might be in trouble  an easement or any sort of restriction  isn't the be-all end-all it is just  something that you want to be aware of  at the very very start rather than  finding out halfway through the process  when you start the discussion with your  architect and they flag a big five metre  easement in the back of your property  and say oh sorry you've just lost 10  percent of your land that you paid x  amount of dollars for on top of that if  you're buying a block of land off the  plan and have no idea what it's going to  look like sight unseen you want to read  that plan or the developers marketing  plan as best as you possibly can there's  a couple things to watch out for first  of all you want to watch out for any  story limits if you want to build a  two-story three-story or even more story  house be sure you can do that on that  block of land in that area  there are some blocks that are zoned  specifically for one story single story  residents some must be two-story which  means you're spending even more money  that you may not be prepared for  or they could have a whole bunch of  other restrictions you also need to be  very very careful and very vigilant  about the block you pick based on where  the light poles are the street trees are  the power dome is going to go and  potentially even where they might  restrict you to put your garage so if  you're restricted to put your garage at  the very back or the very right hand  side of the property this might not work  with what you're trying to achieve so  again it is something that you have to  be very vigilant about and very cautious  of before you commit to purchasing any  blocker land and being caught out the  last thing i want to bring up is bush  fire attack levels now australia has  unfortunately been plagued with numerous  bushfires over the last couple of years  increasing rapidly thanks to climate  change and it is something that we are  all unfortunately going to have to live  with the more and more life progresses  and the more and more we push out into  the bush means we're getting closer and  closer to the bush there are new  regulations called bal or bushfire  attack level regulations that play a  part in the overall block of land  sometimes developers will subdivide a  whole block of land into multiple little  pieces and some bits are right against  that bush which means is exactly what  you're looking for but it might also  increase the construction cost  unfortunately for you in the process  so there's a number of bushfire attack  levels to be mindful of first of all  balo or nobel  is the cheapest and most common that we  are building to in australia  bell 12 and a half bow 19 and bow 29 are  all reasonably acceptable with bow 29  being the highest that we are generally  allowed to build in unless there is  absolutely no other option and then we  really get into the bush land and we get  into the midst of it when we go to bow  40 and the bell fz the bell flame zone  any of these blocks of land will incur  significantly higher construction costs  than what you might be expecting because  they require a safety factor on them  that is simply going to increase the  cost of construction regardless of how  you do it there is no way to basically  build a standard kit home directly in a  flame zone and hope for the best but it  won't be legally allowed you're going to  have to spend a significant amount of  more money to be able to actually  construct to those regulations so if  you're looking at an off the plan grid  and you're looking at all of the  properties available to you  some of them might be pinged as  balo bal29 bow 40 or anything in between  you really want to be looking for the  lowest bow rating or no bail rating at  all if it is possible in your situation  anyway that's all for me today guys  thank you so much for watching i hope  you enjoyed the video if you did make  sure you smash that subscribe button  down below and don't forget about the  like button if you have any questions  leave them in the comments down below i  will answer as many as i possibly can

